# 12. COORDINATION WORKFLOW

Implement an end-to-end emergency coordination workflow.

Example:

An active flood incident is reported.

Step 1 — Context

Retrieve the incident, location, risk assessment, weather observations, affected population, and incident reports.

Step 2 — Task decomposition

The orchestrator determines which specialist agents are needed.

Step 3 — Parallel intelligence

Emergency, environmental, resource, and human-impact agents analyze relevant data in parallel where possible.

Step 4 — Logistics analysis

The logistics agent evaluates feasible warehouses, vehicles, route options and delivery constraints.

Step 5 — Plan synthesis

The response planner combines findings into a structured response proposal.

Step 6 — Verification

The safety agent checks the plan against actual data, permissions and resource availability.

Step 7 — Conflict resolution

The orchestrator identifies contradictions, missing information, infeasible actions and uncertainty.

Step 8 — Response presentation

Present the plan to the authorized emergency commander.

Step 9 — Human decision

The human may approve, modify, reject, request additional analysis or defer.

Step 10 — Controlled execution

Approved actions are executed through existing backend services and transaction-safe APIs.

Step 11 — Monitoring

The monitoring agent tracks meaningful changes and recommends reassessment.

Step 12 — Reassessment

If conditions change significantly, the orchestrator produces an updated plan and clearly explains what changed.

